{{- /*
	object writes a documented object as the pydantic model standing for it. The
	docstring of a class stands inside its body rather than above it, which is
	where Python reads one from, and the docstring of a field stands under the
	field for the same reason.

	A field whose key Python reserves is declared under a name of its own and
	aliased back to the key. The alias names what the class reads and writes, and
	nothing else: the class is configured by nothing, so the name the field
	declares is not a name the class is read by. That is the whole shape for an
	object a response alone carries, which is decoded rather than assembled — and
	it is the wrong shape for one a request carries, whose caller would have to
	name a field Python reserves in order to build it.

	So the shape is pinned rather than made conditional. Writing the other two
	directions now would be writing branches no page exercises and no output
	shows; the assertion stops the generation instead, and sends a person here to
	write the branch against the object that finally needs it.
*/}}
{{- define "object"}}{{/*gotype: github.com/andreychh/tgen/targets/pythonv2.Object*/}}
{{- if .Aliased}}
{{- assert .Direction.Inbound (printf "%s holds an aliased field and no longer travels inbound" .Name)}}
{{- end}}

class {{.Name}}(BaseModel):
    {{.Doc}}
{{- range .Fields}}

    {{.Name}}: {{.Annotation}}{{with .Assignment}} = {{.}}{{end}}
    {{.Doc}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{- /*
	alias writes a name tgen gives a type the documentation leaves unnamed. It is
	a class over the type rather than an assignment to it, because the Go target
	declares a type of its own here and the two targets have to name the same
	things: what Go writes as type RichTextPlain string, Python writes as a model
	whose root is a str, and neither writes the bare built-in.

	The body carries the docstring alone, which is a body: every definition is
	described by prose, so nothing here has to answer for an empty one. A
	documented definition is closed by the link to its section whatever the page
	says about it, and the thirteen tgen introduces are described where they are
	introduced.
*/}}
{{- define "alias"}}{{/*gotype: github.com/andreychh/tgen/targets/pythonv2.Alias*/}}

class {{.Name}}(RootModel[{{.Annotation}}]):
    {{.Doc}}
{{- end}}
